# Hemp storefront — instructions for an LLM agent

Shopify theme for **Hemp**, worked on with the Shopify CLI from this repo root. Read this
before touching anything; most of it is a measurement, not a preference.

## Toolchain (measured 2026-11-21)

| Tool | How | What |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `node` | bare | v22.23.1 — the CLI's own floor is 22.12 |
| `pnpm` | bare | 11.17.0, pinned by `packageManager` in `package.json` |
| `shopify` | `pnpm shopify …` | `@shopify/cli` **5.0.2**, a devDependency. Not global, not on PATH |

`pnpm install` must exit 0 and `pnpm shopify version` must print 5.0.2 before you trust
anything else.

## Layout

```
hemp/
├── hemp-custom/          ← THE THEME. Shopify Skeleton Theme v0.1.0 (MIT), 39 files
├── package.json          @shopify/cli + the theme:* commands
├── shopify.theme.toml    store, theme id and path, per environment
├── pnpm-workspace.yaml   pnpm settings — see "never delete" below
├── .env.example          the CLI's environment variables, documented
├── skincare-commercial/  old-site product scrape + ad media. NOT the storefront
└── .env                  GEMINI_API_KEY for the scripts in skincare-commercial/
```

Shopify's canonical layout puts theme files at the repo root. This repo keeps them in
`hemp-custom/`, so `shopify.theme.toml` carries `path = "./hemp-custom"` — that **is**
honoured by 5.0.2 even though the docs page says `path` is ignored in environments. Do not
"tidy" the theme up to the root without being asked; it would change every command.

## Commands

`pnpm run` lists them. All forwarded arguments go to the CLI:
`pnpm theme:push --unpublished`, `pnpm theme:check --output json`.

| Command | What it does | Needs auth | Needs a store |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `pnpm theme:check` | Theme Check — 39 files, currently **0 offences** | no | no |
| `pnpm theme:dev` | preview with hot reload on a *development* theme | yes | yes |
| `pnpm theme:pull` | store → repo | yes | yes |
| `pnpm theme:push` | repo → store theme | yes | yes |
| `pnpm theme:list` / `info` / `open` / `publish` / `push:unpublished` / `package` | see README | yes | yes |
| `pnpm theme:login` / `theme:logout` | browser session | — | — |

**Why every script is `theme:`-prefixed.** `pnpm list`, `pnpm info`, `pnpm publish` and
`pnpm logout` are *pnpm's own* commands and win over a script of the same name — measured:
`pnpm info --no-input` answered `[ERROR] Unknown option: 'input'` because it ran
`pnpm view`. Never add a script whose bare name collides with a pnpm built-in.

## The two things that will bite

1. **`shopify.theme.toml` ships with a placeholder store** (`your-store.myshopify.com`).
   Until it is edited, every authenticated command fails with *"Please provide a valid
   environment"* — that is the placeholder, not a broken checkout. Precedence is
   **command flag > environment variable > `shopify.theme.toml`**.
2. **The CLI does not read `.env`.** Those variables have to be exported (shell, direnv,
   CI secret). `.env.example` documents them: `SHOPIFY_CLI_THEME_TOKEN` (Theme Access
   password, `shptka_…`), `SHOPIFY_FLAG_STORE`, `SHOPIFY_FLAG_NO_INPUT=1`,
   `SHOPIFY_FLAG_ALLOW_LIVE`.

## The store

`hemp-shack-dev-yxgdumgm.myshopify.com` — a **password-protected development store** (the
storefront answers `302 → /password`), live theme id `191042486514`. The store handle and the
theme path live in `shopify.theme.toml`; the scheme in `store = "https://…"` is tolerated and
normalised by the CLI, though the canonical form is the bare `*.myshopify.com` host.

**Measured 2026-11-21: the storefront is not reliably serving, and the reason is the plan.** A
browser fetched at `/` rendered Shopify's *"Store unavailable — Did you mean …? Start a free trial"*
page while a plain `curl` to the same URL still got the password gate, and the Admin API and theme
push kept working throughout. A store in that state cannot install apps either (the $18 the merchant
was asked for), and it is why the storefront appeared to serve different themes at different times.
Treat "unavailable", "another theme", and "cannot install an app" as one symptom: the store needs a
plan before any of it settles. Do not go hunting the theme again for those.

## Authenticating — read this before debugging a 401

There are two ways in, and they behave differently **in this machine's sandbox**:

1. **Browser login** — `pnpm theme:login` in a terminal. The session lands in
   `~/.config/shopify-cli-kit-nodejs/`. This CLI has **no device-code flow** (`auth login`
   offers only `--no-input` and `--alias`), so it cannot be completed from inside the sandbox.
2. **Theme Access password** — `shptka_…`, per store, from the Theme Access app. This is the
   headless route: put it in `.env` (gitignored) and source it —
   `set -a; . ./.env; set +a; pnpm theme:list`. It needs no login at all.

**The Flatpak trap.** Inside this sandbox `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is
`/var/home/roger/.var/app/bot.cline.app/config`, so the CLI looks for a session in the app's
own config directory and reports *"To run this command, log in to Shopify"* even when the host
has a perfectly good session. Point it at the real one to reuse a terminal login:

```bash
XDG_CONFIG_HOME=/var/home/roger/.config pnpm theme:list
```

Measured 2026-11-21: that override gets past the login check and then failed with
`401 — [API] Service is not valid for authentication` for a session that predates this store.
A 401 is a *token* answer, not a store-address answer — check `--store` normalisation first,
then re-login or switch to a Theme Access password rather than hunting the store name.

**The token route is the one that works here**, measured end to end the same day:

```bash
cd ~/Desktop/hemp && set -a && . ./.env && set +a && pnpm theme:list
```

That answered the theme library and `pnpm theme:push --unpublished --theme "Hemp custom"`
uploaded the theme. The storefront is **password-protected**, so preview links ask for the
storefront password, not an admin login — and no fetch from this sandbox can render the theme
without it. Verification here is Theme Check plus the theme editor, not a screenshot.

**Store facts measured 2026-11-21.** The live theme is **Horizon** (#191042486514), *not* this
repo's Skeleton fork, and there is a development theme from the host machine (#191043567858).
So `theme:pull` brings Horizon down and would replace `hemp-custom/` — only ever pull with
`--theme <id>` after meaning it.

Pushed drafts are named and disposable: `Hemp custom` is #191044026610, created with
`theme:push --unpublished --theme "Hemp custom"` and removable with
`theme:delete --theme 191044026610`.

**`[live]` is not what the browser shows** (measured 2026-11-21, and it cost the
merchant an evening). The theme list said `Hemp custom [live]`, `theme:publish` answered *"already
live"*, and a browser still rendered Horizon — because the tab's URL carried
`?preview_theme_id=191042486514`. A query string outlives the publish, and so does a pinned tab.
Force it with `?preview_theme_id=<our id>` before doubting the publish, and check the address bar
before re-uploading anything.

**Anonymous visitors see Shopify's password page, not ours — and it cannot be switched off.** The
storefront answers `302 → /password` with Shopify's *hosted* screen (no `critical.css`, no
`hemp.css`, `assets/static` only), so the theme's `sections/password.liquid` is not what the public
meets, and `/products.json` is closed behind the same gate. This store is a **client transfer / dev
store**, and Shopify's own limitation list says: *"You can't deactivate private mode for the online
store. You can customize and preview the Password landing page."* So do not send the merchant
hunting for a toggle that is not there — the gate lifts when the store is transferred to the client,
not before.

What works instead: **sign in as staff** (the "Are you the store owner? Log in here" link on the
password page) or use the admin's eye icon beside *Online store*, then browse the real theme;
`?preview_theme_id=<id>` forces a theme for a signed-in visitor. For reading products while the
storefront JSON is closed, an Admin API token is the only route — a Theme Access password cannot
read them.

## Never

- **Never push to the live theme** without being asked in as many words. `theme:push` asks
  first; `--allow-live` / `SHOPIFY_FLAG_ALLOW_LIVE` is the only non-interactive route.
  Do not put it in a script.
- **Never commit** a `shptka_…` token, `.env`, `.shopify/` (CLI session + dev theme id),
  `node_modules/`, `*.zip`. They are already gitignored — keep it that way.
- **Never delete `pnpm-workspace.yaml`.** It holds `allowBuilds: esbuild: true`; without it
  `pnpm install` fails with `ERR_PNPM_IGNORED_BUILDS` and exit 1 (measured).
- **Never invent theme files.** `hemp-custom/` is the Skeleton theme; a missing section or
  template is a design decision, not a gap to fill with a guess.
- **Never touch** `skincare-commercial/` or `.env` as part of storefront work. That folder
  is the old-site scrape (`old site/hempshack-products.csv` + `images/`) and the ad
  media; the storefront does not read it.

## Direction of a sync

`theme:pull` overwrites **local** files with the store's. `theme:push` overwrites the
**store**. Say which one you are about to run before running it, and never run either to
"see what happens".

## History worth knowing

This repo was a WordPress + WooCommerce scaffold until 2026-11-21. `1013d5b`, `f8c339b` and
`aadc936` still hold it — `git show 1013d5b:wordpress/wp-content/themes/hemp/theme.json`.
The one thing worth reusing is the old palette, for the theme's colour scheme:

`#4a5d23` hemp green · `#8a9a5b` sage · `#f5f1e6` cream · `#1f2421` charcoal

There is **no `.github/` and no CI** — nothing deploys automatically. `origin` is
`https://github.com/MarlinChaser2001/hemp.git`, so `git push` publishes the *repository* and
does no store work. A push to Shopify remains a deliberate, interactive act.
